wikipedia_extract: Ōi (大井) was the fourth of five Kuma-class light cruiser, which
  served in the Imperial Japanese Navy during World War II. She was named after the
  Ōi River in Shizuoka prefecture, Japan. Designed as a command vessel for a destroyer
  squadron, she was converted into a torpedo cruiser with forty torpedo launch tubes
  in a plan abandoned by the Japanese Navy in 1942. During most of the Pacific War,
  she was used primarily as a fast troop transport and was sunk by a United States
  Navy submarine in 1944.
